  "executive_briefing": "Global corporate and trade tax policy in 2026 is anchored by the full operationalization of the OECD Pillar Two 15 percent global minimum tax alongside strategic adjustments to national corporate tax rates and tariffs. While Pillar Two rules are actively enforced across Europe, APAC, and ASEAN+3 jurisdictions to neutralize profit shifting, national authorities are recalibrating statutory corporate rates, cash grants, and target rebates\u2014such as Singapore's 50% CIT rebate for YA2026 and Portugal's gradual CIT reductions\u2014to preserve regional competitiveness. In international trade, unilateral tariff measures continue to disrupt supply chains, evidenced by new U.S. tariffs impacting a third of Singapore's U.S. exports. Concurrently, indirect tax administration continues its digital transformation through continuous transaction controls and Peppol-based e-invoicing networks like Singapore's InvoiceNow.",
